Here is a structure proposal for §5.1 (Temperature Model), grounded in what the seven runs and the collected figures actually support. The metrics themselves suggest a clear argumentative arc, so I'll give you the storyline first, then the section skeleton with the figure for each step, then the new figures I'd add.

The argument the data supports

1. Every variant beats the ERA5 reference by a wide margin — skill 0.57–0.71 against bilinear ERA5 (shared, verified-identical baseline), so downscaling works regardless of configuration.
2. The input set is the decisive design choice: atmospheric pressure-level inputs beat the surface configuration on every metric, and — the strongest single finding — they generalise: on the unseen year 2024 all five atmospheric models improve slightly (ΔMAE −0.03 to −0.06 °C) while both surface models degrade (+0.05/+0.06 MAE, +0.11 RMSE).
3. Training length is the biggest single lever: the 60-epoch retrains (clip60) cut MAE by ~0.15 °C over their 30-epoch counterparts. Wind alone hurts at 30 epochs (1.31 vs 1.24) but wins once trained long enough (1.055, the best model overall).
4. Feature importance explains all the ablations: PFI/LIME show the models feed almost entirely on t at 1000/925 hPa at hours 12/15; u/v are nearly unused (why wind doesn't help per se), scaffold/lat/lon/elevation channels are nearly unused (why no-geo ≈ baseline), and the surface model leans on its data channel plus the seasonal encoding (why it fails to transfer).
5. Honest caveats: on 2024 all models drift towards overdispersion (z-std ~0.84–0.90, coverage@90 ~93–94 %) — a side-effect of the between-fold variance term in the ensemble moment match — and the warm tail above ~30 °C is over-predicted by every model.

Proposed skeleton for §5.1

5.1.1 Model variants and evaluation protocol — a small table of the seven runs (label, input set, channels, epochs), one paragraph pointing back to the two regimes of §4.3. No figure.

5.1.2 Headline accuracy and skill — regime_comparison ALL figure plus a compact metrics table (MAE/RMSE/CRPS/skill, CV and 2024 columns). Establishes claims 1 and 3.

5.1.3 Generalisation to the unseen year — the Δ = 2024 − CV story (claim 2). This deserves its own subsection because it is your strongest result; support with perfold_spread (fold spread is small compared with the sfc/atm gap, so the ranking is robust).

5.1.4 Where the error lives — terrain and season. Cross-model: region_bands_mae (sfc error grows with altitude, 1.38→1.52; clip60 models stay ~flat at 0.96–1.17). Then a deep dive on the best model only (atm+wind-clip60): spatial_mae, skill_map, doy_vs_error, mae_vs_elevmismatch. Per-model versions of these go to the appendix.

5.1.5 Calibration and uncertainty — qq_calibration_overlay for CV and 2024 side by side, plus z-std/coverage/MACE rows in the metrics table. State the 2024 overdispersion and attribute it to the between-fold variance in the moment match. Best model's reliability_diagram/uncertainty_vs_error as the single-model view.

5.1.6 Distributional fidelity and the warm tail — distribution_overlay_with_clip60_2024 (density, exceedance, ratio panels). One subsection, one figure: climatology is matched over ~50 °C of range; exceedance above ~30 °C is overestimated by all models.

5.1.7 What the models use — fi_group_importance_pfi (and LIME as corroboration or appendix). Placed after the performance results so it reads as the explanation of 5.1.2–5.1.3, closing the loop on every ablation. Per-model heatmaps → appendix.

5.1.8 Independent station verification — station_regime_comparison (28 NBCN stations). Confirms the ranking on point observations that never entered training; keep the figure's own caveat that pooled station skill is inflated and quote the lowland-overlay numbers instead.

Then your existing Discussion (synthesis: recommend atm+wind-clip60, or atm-clip60 if you value parsimony since wind's contribution is marginal and its channels are unused by PFI; limits: warm tail, 2024 overdispersion) and Conclusion.

This ordering is deliberately: it works → what matters → where it fails → is the uncertainty honest → why → independent check. Each subsection needs exactly one comparison figure plus at most one best-model figure, so the main text stays around 9–10 figures with the 7-model panels in the appendix.

